        orientationTitle: 'Choose the water itinerary only after you choose its return.',
        orientation: 'Kununurra is the useful bed for East Kimberley logistics; Lake Argyle is a separate chapter reached by a named road, booked operator or a combination that the operator confirms. Do not read the regional map as a town shuttle network. Australia’s North West places the lake about 75 km by road from Kununurra via Victoria Highway, while Triple J describes its long Ord cruise as a 55 km one-way journey from the Diversion Dam near town to the Ord Top Dam. These are different products with different departure points: ask whether your ticket includes the transfer back, where bags stay, and when you reach the town bed. A boat cruise is not the same day as a self-drive lake visit, and neither includes every dam lookout or village stop by default. Give the first day to arrival and town orientation. Then choose one lake experience, not a hurried cruise plus the road circuit plus another remote park. Miriima Dawang Woorlab-gerring (Mirima) is a named nearby park option only if its alert, heat conditions, daylight and the group’s mobility suit the chosen track. Keep Purnululu and the Gibb out of this day: each has its own remote access, reservation and return problem.',
        access: 'For a road visit, confirm a vehicle, fuel, accommodation and the exact Lake Argyle Road route before leaving; do not assume that a regional coach, airport transfer or town taxi makes the last mile. For a cruise, read the operator’s current departure point, pickup inclusion, check-in time, duration, cancellation terms, mobility conditions and return transfer in the actual booking. Triple J’s 55 km Ord River product and a Lake Argyle shore cruise are not interchangeable; choose based on the water itinerary you want and the end point your booking names. If you will drive, ask the operator whether the vessel returns to the same place and whether the outing leaves enough daylight for the road. A flight into Kununurra is a separate arrival leg: secure a confirmed ride to the first bed and never place a remote activity before an unprotected same-day flight or vehicle hand-back. Read Main Roads status, BOM forecasts and local water-safety guidance at the date of travel. If any leg cannot be verified, remain in the confirmed town base.',
        sequence: 'Arrive, collect supplies and sleep in Kununurra before setting a remote departure. On the first full day, use a short town orientation or one open Mirima route; select the trail from the live park page, carry water and turn back when heat or footing changes. Put the lake on a separate day: confirm whether the plan is a booked cruise, a self-drive visit to the village and dam outlook, or an operator transfer linking both. Read the ticket’s start, finish, meals, accessibility and cancellation terms, then pin the return bed and leave enough slack for the actual service. Take only the one formal viewpoint or town stop that fits the chosen product. Use the following morning as a weather or delay buffer before a flight, long road or separate East Kimberley leg. If wind, heat, a road notice or operator cancellation removes the lake activity, stay in town and choose only a currently open local visit; do not improvise a swim, cross a closed road or substitute an unverified Purnululu drive.',
        interpretation: 'Kununurra lies on Miriwoong Country. Use Miriwoong-led cultural interpretation where it is offered, learn place names from local sources, and treat the Ord system as a living place with communities, work and Country—not an empty reservoir backdrop.',
        boundary: 'Respect Miriwoong Country, private property, water infrastructure and all signed access controls. Lake and river water can contain hazards including freshwater crocodiles; a scenic photo, warm day or other visitor entering the water is not safety advice. Swim only where the responsible local authority explicitly permits it at that time. Do not launch a boat, cross an agricultural or dam facility, or enter a cultural place without the named operator’s permission.',
